  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, please have a seat. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S009",
      "patient_response": "Well, thanks, doc. I appreciate it. I'll just get right to it, because, well, it's getting to be a real problem and I just don't know what's going on with me lately. What brings me in is I just keep forgetting things. And I don't mean normal things, like, you know, where I set my coffee mug down or where my keys are—I mean things I really, absolutely shouldn’t be forgetting. Important things. \n\nLike my heart follow-up appointments, for example. The cardiologist makes a big deal about it, says I need to come in to check on things, and I just completely miss it. Or the clinic will leave a message, and I think to myself, 'Okay, I need to call the clinic back right after lunch,' and then... nothing. It just completely vanishes from my mind until someone yells at me about it weeks later. \n\nAnd it's not just the medical stuff either, which is bad enough, believe me, but it's my everyday stuff too. Since I stopped working, I try to keep busy, do some repair jobs around the house or help out the neighbors, but I'll show up to fix something and realize I didn't bring the right tools. I'll get all the way under a sink or walk over to a buddy's garage, and I've got the completely wrong wrench, or I left the whole toolbox sitting on my kitchen table. It's just so frustrating, you know? You get all geared up to do a simple job, you know exactly what needs to be done, and your brain just skips a track and you're standing there empty-handed. I just can't keep dropping the ball on these things, so I figured I had to come talk to somebody.",
