    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "About how long have you been experiencing these problems with your memory?",
      "targeted_symptom": "duration",
      "patient_response": "Well, let me think on that. I’d say it’s been causing real, actual trouble for about a year and two months now. Give or take a couple of weeks, but right around fourteen months. Because, see, that’s exactly when I had my cardiac scare. Oh boy, that was a whole ordeal. You think you’re perfectly fine, just going about your day, and suddenly the doctors are all hovering over you telling you that you need to take it easy. And I mean, I get it, they’re just doing their jobs, but taking it easy? That is just not how I’m wired. \n\nBefore the heart thing, I was always, always on the move. I’d be out walking for miles every single morning, rain or shine, just wearing out the soles of my boots, or I’d be elbow-deep in these big projects. I’d be fixing up old engines, building shelves, helping the guys down the street with whatever they needed. And doing all that... it used to keep me settled, you know? Like, as long as my hands and my feet were busy, my brain worked just fine. It kept me grounded. \n\nBut then this heart scare happens, and they forced me to cut way, way back on the walking and the projects. 'Sit in your recliner,' they said. 'Rest.' And I swear to you, doc, the minute I had to stop moving so much, it was like a switch flipped. Without all that activity to burn off whatever it is inside me, my brain just started spinning out of control and I started forgetting every little thing. So yeah, about a year and two months of this nonsense, all because I can't just get up and go like I used to.",
