    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Thinking back to when you were a kid or in school, did you ever have trouble with paying attention, being restless, or staying organized?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, now that you mention it... yeah. Yeah, I suppose I did. I mean, it’s funny you bring that up because I haven't thought about grade school in, well, decades. But, oh boy, my poor mother. She used to get so frustrated with me. I was that kid, you know? The one the teachers were always keeping an eye on. I just... I could not for the life of me stay in my seat. They’d tell us to sit and read, or practice our penmanship, and I’d be up sharpening my pencil for the fifth time, or looking out the window, or walking over to see what Billy was doing two desks down. It was like the chair was made of hot coals. \n\nAnd the talking! I was always, always getting notes sent home pinned to my shirt because I was talking too much. 'He disrupts the class,' they’d write. Or 'He lacks self-control.' The teacher would ask a question to the whole room, and before she even finished the sentence, I’d just blurt out the answer. I didn't mean to be a nuisance, I really didn't, but my brain would just jump to the answer and my mouth would say it before I even remembered I was supposed to raise my hand. It was just... it was like I had a motor inside me that was always running a little too fast, even way back then. But, you know, back then they didn't really do anything about it, they just told you to straighten up or gave you a swat, and eventually, I just figured out how to keep myself busy with work and sports so I wouldn't get in trouble. But yeah, to answer your question, it was definitely an issue when I was a boy.",
